  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 213 PM CDT Tue Sep 29 2026\n\nThe first round of rain this morning through early this afternoon \ncontinued to move off to the northeast, resulting in fairly \nwidespread 0.20-0.30\" rainfall, mainly west of Highway 283. Another \nsmall corridor of enhanced rainfall moved across portions of south \ncentral Kansas yielding similar amounts, locally around 1/2\". MRMS \nReflectivity at Lowest Altitude product showed a distinct break in \nthe activity from northeastern New Mexico into the Texas and \nOklahoma Panhandle as of 1830Z, so we should see a fairly large area \nof southwest Kansas seeing a multiple hour break in rainfall later \nthis afternoon before the main rainfall event arrives tonight.\n\nThe ongoing Flood Watch remains in effect for much of southwest and \nall of west central Kansas, mainly due to the flash flooding risk \nfrom the main event later tonight through Wednesday morning. The \nstage is set for heavy rainfall, and the DDC 18Z sounding confirmed \na highly anomalous 1.74\" Precipitable Water (PW), very much \nsupportive of excessive rainfall rates later on tonight. This PW \nfalls well above the 99th percentile of all observed DDC upper air \nsoundings with only a handful of observations in history showing \nhigher PW from roughly mid-September to early October. Given the \ntropical characteristics of the 18Z sounding very much aligning with \nmodeled PW, we feel even more confident of excessive rainfall \ntonight when the combined remnants of Hurricane Polo and the \nincoming upper level low tied to, but cutoff from, the polar jet \nstream. Thus, see no reason to deviate much from the fairly \naggressive QPF forecast and counties in the Flood Watch for tonight \nthrough Thursday. \n\nThe tricky part of the forecast is the incoming dry slot tied to the \neventual maturation of the extra-tropical cyclone (ETC) on \nWednesday. The dry intrusion will likely move rapidly north during \nthe day Wednesday across western Kansas, so heavy rain will shift \nfairly quickly to the north during the day Wednesday. In fact, we \nwill probably see some sunshine by afternoon, leading to a \ndestabilization of the atmosphere for renewed surface-based \nconvection. By Wednesday afternoon, the surface low will likely \nremain anchored over southeastern Colorado, and this is likely the \nbest location for the most aggressive surface-based convection, \nincluding the possibility of an isolated tornado or two. Surface-\nbased severe risk will extend northeastward along an arc across west \ncentral into portions of southwest Kansas where local convergence is \nenhanced along a northwest to southeast surface wind confluence \naxis. Any new surface-based storms along this arc will be capable of \na tornado given the low Lifted Condensation Level (LCL) environment \nand 0-1km SRH in the 100-200 m2/s2 range. \n\nThe entire ETC will undergo fairly rapid occlusion late Wednesday \nand Wednesday Night, and after Wednesday Night ,the mid level \ncyclone will begin its spin-down/weakening phase as we head into \nThursday, and latest models suggest the 500mb low center will track \ndue east across southwest Kansas with an associated 700mb level \ndeformation axis just north of the 500mb low track. This is where \nadditional rainfall of 1/2 to 1 inch is likely to occur, hence the \nreason the Flood Watch continues through the day Thursday.",
